fully using short, manageable strings. I'm listing out concrete improvements like fixing the title typo, adding a header and tagline, adding a CTA button, styling the list as cards, and adding meta tags for SEO and mobile usability, each paired with a brief rationale.\n\nI'm shortening the What/Why text pairs for each improvement to make the hash computation more manageable, settling on brief phrases like \"Fix typo\" and \"Builds trust\" rather than longer descriptive text, though I recognize even shortened strings require many careful modular arithmetic steps to compute by hand accurately.\n\nI'll settle on moderately descriptive What/Why pairs and hash those combined strings, being systematic about it: fixing the typo, adding the header/tagline, adding a CTA, styling the book list as cards, and adding meta viewport/description tags for mobile and SEO gains.\n\nActually, six pairs means too many hashing steps to do reliably by hand, so I'm trimming down to four shorter, punchier improvements—fixing the title typo, adding a hero CTA, styling the book list, and adding meta tags—each with concise What/Why text to keep the hashing manageable, though I'm now leaning toward five for better quality coverage.\n\nSettling on five improvements: title typo fix, hero CTA, book list styling, meta tags, and an email signup form, each paired with a short justification. Now I'm starting the DJB2 hash computation character by character, tracking ASCII values for each letter to build up the hash incrementally.\n\nContinuing to grind through the hash computation, multiplying by 33 and adding each character code, reducing modulo 2^32 at each step to keep the running total in range.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through h9, h10, h11, applying the multiply-by-33 and mod 2^32 steps successively.\n\nContinuing the hash computation, I get h14=2096302138, h15=458493884, h16=2245396401, and I'm now working through h17 by multiplying and adding the next character code before applying the modulus reduction.\n\nContinuing the hash computation: h20 = 205513917, h21 = 2486991997, h22 = 466357393, and now working through h23 with more multiply-and-mod steps...\n\nh23 = 2504892195\nh24 = 1057063928\nh25 computing: 1057063928*33 + 115 = 34883109739, mod 8*4294967296 = 34359738368, still reducing.\n\nContinuing hash calculation, h25 = 523371371, h26 = 91386175. Now converting 91386175 to hex.\n\nSum checks out to 91386175, confirming the hex value 0x0572713f. I'll form the class name as \"HDLmClass572713f\" using this hash, then move to hashing the next string, \"Add hero CTABoosts clicks\", by breaking it into its character codes.\n\nStill computing the DJB2 hash...\n\nContinuing the hash computation through h9, h10, h11, and now h12, applying the multiply-by-33 and mod 2^32 steps each time.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through h18...\n\nh18 = 273551118\nh19 = 437252334\nh20 = 1544425233\nh21, still reducing mod 4294967296...\n\nContinuing the mod computation, h24 becomes 3287168084, then computing h25 gives 1102364487, and now I'm converting that final hash value to hexadecimal by dividing out successive powers of 16.\n\nThat confirms the second hash value. Now I'm computing the djb2 hash for the third string, \"Style book listBetter UX\", walking through each character's ASCII value and accumulating the hash step by step.\n\nContinuing the modular hash computation, I get h7=272775416, h8=411654247, h9=699688374, and h10=1614879969.\n\nContinuing the rolling hash computation, I get h13, h14, h15 through successive multiply-add-mod steps, and I'm now starting the calculation for h16.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through h19 to h22, applying the multiply-by-33 and mod reduction pattern at each step.\n\nh22 = 696024579\nh23 = 1493974712\nh24 = 2056525328\n\nNow I'm converting 2056525328 to hexadecimal by dividing out successive powers of 16, finding the leading digit is 7 and continuing to extract the next digit.\n\nContinuing the hex conversion, I get digits a, 9, 4, 1, 6, 1, 0, giving 0x7a941610, and I double check by summing the place values back together.\n\nNow I move to hashing the fourth string \"Add meta tagsSEO boost\", computing each character's ASCII value and running through the same iterative hash steps, tracking h0 through h4.\n\nStill computing hash values step by step...\n\nContinuing the hash computation through h15, h16, h17, and starting h18, applying the multiply-by-33-plus-char-code step with modular reduction at each stage.\n\nContinuing the hash computation: h21 = 4237774398, then h22 = 2407601778. Now I'm converting this final hash value to hexadecimal by dividing out successive powers of 16.\n\nConfirmed, so Hash4 is HDLmClass8f811672. Now I'm computing the hash for string 5, \"Add email signupCaptures leads,\" breaking it into its 30 characters and running the djb2-style computation through several rounds to get intermediate hash values.\n\nContinuing the modular hash computation, working through h8 to h11 with each step multiplying by 33, adding the character code, and reducing modulo powers of 2^32.\n\nContinuing the hash computation: h14=715683815, then h15 comes out to 2142729532, h16 to 1990597932, h17 to 1265222383, and now I'm working through h18...\n\nContinuing the hash computation, I get h21=2677456649, h22=2456723611, h23=3762467936, and I'm starting h24's calculation.\n\nContinuing the hash computation...\n\nh30 = 2336868828, and now I'm converting this value to hexadecimal digit by digit.\n\nI get 0x8b49c9dc, verify it checks out, giving Hash5 = HDLmClass8b49c9dc. Now I'm moving on to build the improved HTML page, since the original appears to be a bare page with just an image and links.\n\nI'll add a proper head with title, meta description, and viewport, then a header with an h1 reading \"The Wizard of Oz Books by L. Frank Baum\" plus a tagline tied to the hero CTA improvement. I'll fix the \"Wixard\" typo, add a \"Start Reading Book 1 Free\" CTA button, style the 