tribution gets folded into the hash.\n\nContinuing the division to extract each hex digit, getting D2118743, then verifying by reconstructing the value digit by digit to confirm the conversion is correct.\n\nNow I'm computing a second hash for \"Email signup added\" paired with \"Captures leads\" using the same djb2-style algorithm, character by character.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through the remaining characters, tracking each multiply-add-modulo step for n, u, p, space, a, and d...\n\nContinuing through characters 23-27, computing the running hash values for \"u\", \"r\", \"e\", \"s\", and the space character.\n\nFinishing the hex conversion, I get A1AF7E65, verified against the running total. That gives me Hash3 = HDLmClassA1AF7E65, and now I'm moving to Improvement 4, computing the hash for the \"Fixed Wixard typo\" / \"Builds trust\" string by starting to sum character codes.\n\nContinuing the hash computation for characters x, a, r...\n\nWorking through subsequent letters d, sp, t, y, p, o with the same multiply-add-reduce pattern, tracking the running difference values after each modulus reduction.\n\nContinuing through characters t, r, u, s, t, landing on a running value of 2794081429, and now I'm starting to convert that final number into hexadecimal by dividing down through the place values.\n\nWorking through the remaining division steps, I get the hex digits A, 6, 8, A, 4, C, 9, 5, giving me A68A4C95, and I verify by summing back through all the place values to confirm it equals 2794081429. So Hash4 comes out to HDLmClassA68A4C95.\n\nNow moving to Improvement 5, pairing \"Mobile styles\" with \"Most users are mobile,\" I'm checking the combined string length and considering shortening the \"Why\" to \"Mobile traffic\" to see how that changes the character count.\n\nThat combination still felt off, so I switched to \"Responsive styles\" with \"Mobile friendly,\" giving a 32-character string, and I'm now computing the DJB2-style hash by walking through each character's ASCII value and applying the multiply-by-33 and overflow wraparound at each step.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through the remaining characters, tracking each multiply-add-mod step for \"sp\", \"s\", \"t\", and \"y\" in sequence.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through characters i, l, e, sp, f...\n\nContinuing the multiplication step, I get diff=3206910668, then converting this to hex digit by digit through repeated division, landing on B, F, 2, 5, 9, 2, C as the sequence of hex characters.\n\nThe final hex works out to BF2592CC, which I verify by summing back the components, confirming it matches 3206910668 exactly. This gives Hash5 as \"HDLmClassBF2592CC\".\n\nNow moving to improvement 6, I'm computing the character codes for the string \"Buy books sectionAdds revenue\" as part of building the next hash.\n\nWorking through the djb2-style hash step by step for this new string, multiplying by 33 and adding each character code, wrapping with modulo 2^32 when values overflow.\n\nStill working through the hash computation, letter by letter...\n\nContinuing the character-by-character hash computation for \"u\" and \"e\", getting a difference of 2116141982, then I'm converting that number to hexadecimal by successive division.\n\nI get 7E21C39E and verify it sums back correctly, giving me Hash6 = HDLmClass7E21C39E.\n\nNow I'm drafting Improvement 7 with \"What\" and \"Why\" text, then starting to compute character codes for the new string to hash it too.\n\nContinuing the hash computation, working through each character of \"new readers\" to derive the running hash value step by step through characters e, r, sp, C, T...\n\nA, G, u, i, d, e, s.\n\nContinuing with \"sp\": 261312272*33=8623304976; +32=8623305008; subtracting 2*4294967296 gives 33370416.\n23. n: 33370416*33=1101223728; +110=1101223838\n\nContinuing the letter-by-letter modular computation through \"e\", \"w\", \"sp\", \"r\", \"e\", \"a\", \"d\", tracking each step's multiply-add-mod cycle to derive the running difference values.\n\nNow converting the final numeric result into hexadecimal by successively dividing out powers of 16.\n\nVerified the hex works out to 3A8E4BC0, giving me Hash7 = HDLmClass3A8E4BC0. That's 7 improvements done, so now I'm moving on to writing the actual HTML content.\n\nI need to keep all the book links, fix the \"Wixard\" typo to \"Wonderful Wizard,\" and build out a hero section, card grid, CTA, email signup, buy section, and footer with responsive styling. I'll structure cards for each book with year and \"Read free\" labels, plus include the play PDF and Little Wizard Stories.\n\nWriting the HTML structure...\n\nRounding out the list through Rinkitink in Oz, The Lost Princess of Oz, The Tin Woodman of Oz, The Magic of Oz, and Glinda of Oz, completing all fourteen original books. Now I'll write it up.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Since I don't have the raw HTML, I'll need to reconstruct a reasonable version while preserving the content, then compute DJB2 hashes manually by processing each character through the standard hash*33+c formula for the improvement strings I choose.\n\nI'm opting for shorter What/Why pairs to minimize error in hand-computing the hashes, sketching out several candidate improvements like a hero intro, book cards, an email signup, responsive styles, a fixed typo, a clear CTA, and possibly shop links, though I'm wary about inventing revenue links that weren't in the original.\n\nI decide to add a legitimate Amazon search link for print editions rather than a fake affiliate link, then begin manually computing DJB2 hashes character by character for the first improvement string.\n\nStill computing hash values...\n\nContinuing through characters i, n, t, r, o, and space, applying the multiply-by-33-add-charcode-mod-2^32 hash computation step by step to each letter.\n\nContinuing the modular hash computation through each character of the string, tracking the running remainder at every step.\n\nContinuing the rolling hash computation through \"value\" and into the next space character, tracking each multiply-add-mod step.\n\nI finish computing the last hash step and get 2227935246, then convert this to hexadecimal digit by digit, working out each nibble through repeated division.\n\nComputing next hash...\n\nStill computing the hash through each remaining character...\n\nContinuing through the character-by-character encoding calculations, working through steps 21 to 27, tracking the running difference values as each letter's contribution gets folded into the hash.\n\nContinuing the division to extract each hex digit, getting D2118743, then verifying by reconstructing the value digit by digit to confirm the conversion is correct.\n\nNow I'm computing a second hash for \"Email signup added\" paired with \"Captures leads\" using the same djb2-style algorithm, character by character.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through the remaining characters, tracking each multiply-add-modulo step for n, u, p, space, a, and d...\n\nContinuing through characters 23-27, computing the running hash values for \"u\", \"r\", \"e\", \"s\", and the space character.\n\nFinishing the hex conversion, I get A1AF7E65, verified against the running total. That gives me Hash3 = HDLmClassA1AF7E65, and now I'm moving to Improvement 4, computing the hash for the \"Fixed Wixard typo\" / \"Builds trust\" string by starting to sum character codes.\n\nContinuing the hash computation for characters x, a, r...\n\nWorking through subsequent letters d, sp, t, y, p, o with the same multiply-add-reduce pattern, tracking the running difference values after each modulus reduction.\n\nContinuing through characters t, r, u, s, t, landing on a running value of 2794081429, and now I'm starting to convert that final number into hexadecimal by dividing down through the place values.\n\nWorking through the remaining division steps, I get the hex digits A, 6, 8, A, 4, C, 9, 5, giving me A68A4C95, and I verify by summing back through all the place values to confirm it equals 2794081429. So Hash4 comes out to HDLmClassA68A4C95.\n\nNow moving to Improvement 5, pairing \"Mobile styles\" with \"Most users are mobile,\" I'm checking the combined string length and considering shortening the \"Why\" to \"Mobile traffic\" to see how that changes the character count.\n\nThat combination still felt off, so I switched to \"Responsive styles\" with \"Mobile friendly,\" giving a 32-character string, and I'm now computing the DJB2-style hash by walking through each character's ASCII value and applying the multiply-by-33 and overflow wraparound at each step.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through the remaining characters, tracking each multiply-add-mod step for \"sp\", \"s\", \"t\", and \"y\" in sequence.\n\nContinuing the hash computation through characters i, l, e, sp, f...\n\nContinuing the multiplication step, I get diff=3206910668, then converting this to hex digit by digit through repeated division, landing on B, F, 2, 5, 9, 2, C as the sequence of hex characters.\n\nThe final hex works out to BF2592CC, which I verify by summing back the components, confirming it matches 3206910668 exactly. This gives Hash5 as \"HDLmClassBF2592CC\".\n\nNow moving to improvement 6, I'm computing the character codes for the string \"Buy books sectionAdds revenue\" as part of building the next hash.\n\nWorking through the djb2-style hash step by step for this new string, multiplying by 33 and adding each character code, wrapping with modulo 2^32 when values overflow.\n\nStill working through the hash computation, letter by letter...\n\nContinuing the character-by-character hash computation for \"u\" and \"e\", getting a difference of 2116141982, then I'm converting that number to hexadecimal by successive division.\n\nI get 7E21C39E and verify it sums back correctly, giving me Hash6 = HDLmClass7E21C39E.\n\nNow I'm drafting Improvement 7 with \"What\" and \"Why\" text, then starting to compute character codes for the new string to hash it too.\n\nContinuing the hash computation, working through each character of \"new readers\" to derive the running hash value step by step through characters e, r, sp, C, T...\n\nA, G, u, i, d, e, s.\n\nContinuing with \"sp\": 261312272*33=8623304976; +32=8623305008; subtracting 2*4294967296 gives 33370416.\n23. n: 33370416*33=1101223728; +110=1101223838\n\nContinuing the letter-by-letter modular computation through \"e\", \"w\", \"sp\", \"r\", \"e\", \"a\", \"d\", tracking each step's multiply-add-mod cycle to derive the running difference values.\n\nNow converting the final numeric result into hexadecimal by successively dividing out powers of 16.\n\nVerified the hex works out to 3A8E4BC0, giving me Hash7 = HDLmClass3A8E4BC0. That's 7 improvements done, so now I'm moving on to writing the actual HTML content.\n\nI need to keep all the book links, fix the \"Wixard\" typo to \"Wonderful Wizard,\" and build out a hero section, card grid, CTA, email signup, buy section, and footer with responsive styling. I'll structure cards for each book with year and \"Read free\" labels, plus include the play PDF and Little Wizard Stories.\n\nWriting the HTML structure...\n\nRounding out the list through Rinkitink in Oz, The Lost Princess of Oz, The Tin Woodman of Oz, The Magic of Oz, and Glinda of Oz, completing all fourteen original books. Now I'll write it up.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":4382,"completion_tokens":20534,"total_tokens":24916,"cost":0.53526,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.53526,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.02191,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.51335},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":14105,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0},"server_tool_use_details":{"web_search_requests":0}}}
